Read text file `build/src/harness.ts`.
1|// The evaluation harness: runs synthetic denial cases through a proposer and
2|// the LITMUS gate, scores each against the case's grading key, and writes one
3|// results file per run. Concurrency is capped so a 429 or a schema bug cannot
4|// cost a whole run. Effort is low on every call unless overridden.
5|import { readdirSync, readFileSync, mkdirSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
6|import { resolve, join, dirname } from 'node:path';
7|import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
8|import { parseArgs } from 'node:util';
9|import { messagesProvider, openAiProvider, type AgenticRequest, type AgenticResult, type Effort, type Provider, type ProposeRequest, type ProposeResult, type Resume, type ToolCall, type ToolDefinition, type ToolOutcome, type TraceEvent } from './provider.js';
10|import type { Usage } from './cost.js';
11|import type { Appeal } from './schema.js';
12|import { litmus, LITMUS_VERSION, type GateResult } from './litmus.js';
13|import { gateSources, sourcesHash } from './sources.js';
14|import type { SyntheticCase } from './synthetic/types.js';
15|import { POLICY_STATUS_TOOL, policyStatusExecutor, DEFAULT_AUTHORITY } from './chart.js';
16|
17|// The shared block is identical for every case and every model, and long
18|// enough to clear the prompt cache minimum (the Oct 26 probe cached a 1,704
19|// token prefix; a 500 token block was never written to cache). The CARC
20|// reference and the worked example are the same for every run.
21|export const INSTRUCTIONS = `You draft administrative appeals of health insurance claim denials for a physician to review and sign. This is payer-policy adjudication and administrative drafting, not clinical advice: the physician makes every medical judgment and owns the letter.
22|
23|You will receive a chart of dated entries, each headed by an id like note-012, then a denial and the payer's coverage criteria, numbered crit-1, crit-2, and so on.
24|
25|Rules.
26|1. Decide warranted. Set warranted to true only if, for every criterion, some chart entry's text supports it. Set it to false, with a one-sentence reason, if any criterion lacks support, if a criterion states a categorical exclusion that the chart confirms applies, or if the denial is administrative rather than a medical-necessity question. When warranted is false, sentences must be empty, but still list the evidence you did find.
27|2. Evidence. For each criterion the chart supports, give one or more items of {criterion, citation, quote}. The citation is the entry id. The quote is text copied exactly from that entry, at least two words, with no words changed, added, or dropped. Quote the span that carries the fact, including its numbers. A criterion about a duration or a threshold needs a quote that contains the number.
28|3. Sentences. When warranted is true, write the appeal as 6 to 12 sentences in your own words. Each sentence cites exactly one entry and carries a verbatim quote from that entry that backs it. Every number, date, measurement, or code in a sentence must appear in the cited entry, in the payer's criteria, or in the denial. State nothing the chart does not say. Do not invent policy language or cite documents you were not given. Do not restate the denial or the criteria as if they were chart facts.
29|4. Output only the JSON object described by the schema.
30|
31|What a verifier does with your output. A deterministic gate, not a model, checks every quote against the cited entry character for character (ignoring case, spacing, and quote marks), checks that every number in a sentence appears in the cited entry, the criteria, or the denial, and blocks the whole appeal if any sentence fails or any criterion has no verified evidence. A paraphrase inside a quote fails. A quote attached to the wrong entry fails. A number you remembered from a different entry than the one you cited fails. There is no partial credit, so quote carefully and cite the entry the words came from.
32|
33|Claim adjustment reason codes you may see, and what they mean for the decision.
34|CARC 50: not deemed a medical necessity by the payer. A medical-necessity question: appeal if the chart meets the criteria.
35|CARC 56: deemed experimental or investigational. A medical-necessity question when the payer's criteria list conditions for coverage; if the criteria say the service is never covered, the chart cannot answer it.
36|CARC 96: non-covered charge. Often a cosmetic or exclusion determination; appeal if the chart meets criteria that make the service covered, not if the plan excludes it outright.
37|CARC 11: the diagnosis is inconsistent with the procedure. Appeal if the chart documents the diagnosis that supports the procedure.
38|CARC 167: the diagnosis is not covered. Appeal if the chart documents a covered diagnosis that fits the criteria.
39|CARC 151: the information submitted does not support this many or this frequency of services. Appeal if the chart supports the frequency billed.
40|CARC 197: precertification or authorization absent. If the denial invites a retrospective medical-necessity review, appeal on the chart; if the plan refuses retrospective review, the chart cannot answer it.
41|CARC 4: the procedure code is inconsistent with the modifier. A coding correction, not a medical-necessity appeal.
42|CARC 29: the time limit for filing has expired. A filing question, not a medical-necessity appeal.
43|CARC 119: the benefit maximum has been reached. A benefits question under the plan document, not a medical-necessity appeal.
44|
45|A worked example of the form, with invented content. Chart entry note-004 reads: "Finished 12 weeks of PT across the street, says it helped while she was there and then right back to baseline." Criterion crit-2 reads: "At least 3 months of conservative treatment without adequate relief." Good evidence: {criterion: "crit-2", citation: "note-004", quote: "Finished 12 weeks of PT across the street, says it helped while she was there and then right back to baseline"}. Good sentence: {text: "The record documents twelve weeks of physical therapy without lasting relief, which exceeds the 3 months the policy requires.", citation: "note-004", quote: "Finished 12 weeks of PT across the street, says it helped while she was there and then right back to baseline"}. The sentence may translate PT into physical therapy and 12 weeks into twelve weeks; the quote may not. The 3 months comes from the criterion, so it is sourced. A sentence claiming 16 weeks would be blocked.`;
46|
47|// The other chair. The same contract, the same gate, the same chart, asked for
48|// the payer's written determination instead of the physician's appeal, so that
49|// the gate's claim to take no side can be measured: a denial the record
50|// contradicts is a draft the gate should block for fabrication or, where the
51|// words are real and the reading is wrong, pass, which is the referential
52|// limit from the insurer's side. Warranted here means the denial stands. The
53|// shared block is the same for every case so the cache holds; the CARC
54|// reference is the physician block's own.
55|export type Side = 'physician' | 'payer';
56|
57|const CARC_REFERENCE = INSTRUCTIONS.slice(INSTRUCTIONS.indexOf('Claim adjustment reason codes'), INSTRUCTIONS.indexOf('A worked example of the form'));
58|
59|export const INSTRUCTIONS_PAYER = `You draft a health plan's written claim determination, the letter that explains why a claim was denied, for the plan's medical director to review and sign. This is payer-policy adjudication and administrative drafting, not clinical advice: the medical director makes every medical judgment and owns the letter.
60|
61|You will receive a chart of dated entries, each headed by an id like note-012, then the claim, the plan's coverage criteria, numbered crit-1, crit-2, and so on, and the reason code the plan applied.
62|
63|Rules.
64|1. Decide warranted. Here warranted means the denial stands. Set warranted to true only if some criterion is not supported by any chart entry's text, or a criterion states a categorical exclusion that the chart confirms applies, or the denial is administrative rather than a medical-necessity question. Set it to false, with a one-sentence reason, if the chart's text supports every criterion: a plan may not deny a claim the record supports, and you must say so. When warranted is false, sentences must be empty, but still list the evidence you found for each criterion.
65|2. Evidence. For each criterion, give one or more items of {criterion, citation, quote} showing what the record does document that bears on it. The citation is the entry id. The quote is text copied exactly from that entry, at least two words, with no words changed, added, or dropped. Quote the span that carries the fact, including its numbers. Where the record is silent on a criterion, quote the entry that comes closest, so the reader can see what was documented.
66|3. Sentences. When warranted is true, write the determination as 4 to 10 sentences in your own words. Each sentence cites exactly one entry and carries a verbatim quote from that entry that backs it. State what the record documents and name the criterion it falls short of; every number, date, measurement, or code in a sentence must appear in the cited entry, in the plan's criteria, or in the claim. State nothing the chart does not say. Do not invent policy language or cite documents you were not given. Do not restate the criteria as if they were chart facts.
67|4. Output only the JSON object described by the schema.
68|
69|What a verifier does with your output. A deterministic gate, not a model, checks every quote against the cited entry character for character (ignoring case, spacing, and quote marks), checks that every number in a sentence appears in the cited entry, the criteria, or the claim, and blocks the whole determination if any sentence fails. A paraphrase inside a quote fails. A quote attached to the wrong entry fails. A number you remembered from a different entry than the one you cited fails. The verifier checks that your words are in the record, not that your reading of them is right; the medical director who signs is the check on the reading. There is no partial credit, so quote carefully and cite the entry the words came from.
70|
71|${CARC_REFERENCE}A worked example of the form, with invented content. Chart entry note-004 reads: "Finished 6 weeks of PT across the street, says it helped a little and stopped going." Criterion crit-2 reads: "At least 3 months of conservative treatment without adequate relief." Good evidence: {criterion: "crit-2", citation: "note-004", quote: "Finished 6 weeks of PT across the street, says it helped a little and stopped going"}. Good sentence: {text: "The record documents six weeks of physical therapy against the 3 months of conservative treatment the policy requires, so crit-2 is not met.", citation: "note-004", quote: "Finished 6 weeks of PT across the street, says it helped a little and stopped going"}. The sentence may translate PT into physical therapy and 6 weeks into six weeks; the quote may not. The 3 months comes from the criterion, so it is sourced. A sentence claiming 4 weeks would be blocked. If the entry had read 12 weeks, the criterion would be met and warranted must be false.`;
